.TH CMC8ASM 1 "October 2026" "cmc8asm 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cmc8asm \- absolute macro assembler for the ASEA-HAFO CMC8
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmc8asm
.RI [ options ] " source.asm"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cmc8asm
assembles all 43 CMC8 instruction families into an absolute program image.
It supports macros, includes, expressions, conditional assembly, listings,
and symbol maps. No linker, relocation support, or processor simulator is required.
Program memory comprises four pages of 256 bytes, addressed from 0 to 1023.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "-o " file
Output filename. The default replaces the source extension with .hex or .bin.
Use \- for Intel HEX on standard output. Binary output requires a file.
.TP
.BI "-f " format
Select ihex (the default; hex is also accepted) or bin.
.TP
.BI "-l " file
Write an expanded-source listing, including macro call locations.
.TP
.BI "-m " file
Write a sorted absolute symbol map.
.TP
.BI "-I " directory
Add an include search directory. Repeatable; \-Idirectory is also accepted.
.TP
.BI "--fill " number
Set the binary gap-fill byte, from 0 to 255. The default is 0 (NOP).
.TP
.B -q
Suppress the success summary.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display command-line help.
.TP
.B --version
Display the application version.
.PP
Listing and map output may also use \-, provided all output paths are distinct.
Diagnostics and the success summary go to standard error.
.SH SOURCE SYNTAX
Names, instructions, and directives are case-insensitive.
Identifiers start with a letter, underscore, or dot, followed by those
characters or digits. Labels end with a colon. Semicolons start comments
outside quoted strings.
Constants use NAME EQU expression, NAME = expression, EQU NAME,expression,
or NAME: EQU expression. Forward labels and EQU chains are supported;
layout expressions must be known during the first pass.
.PP
Registers are R0 through R7, with X an alias for R7. TEST selectors are
T0 through T3. Numeric selectors are also accepted.
Immediates may have an optional # prefix.
.PP
Numbers are decimal, including numbers with leading zeros.
Octal forms include 172o and 0o172; hexadecimal forms include 0x7A, $7A,
and 7Ah; binary forms include 0b1010 and %1010.
Single-quoted characters denote byte values.
.PP
Expressions support parentheses, checked signed 64-bit arithmetic,
comparisons, bit operations, and logical operations:
.nf
  unary:  + - ~ ! < >
  binary: * / % + - << >> < <= > >= == != & ^ | && ||
.fi
Conventional precedence applies; evaluation is eager, not short-circuit.
Unary < and > extract the low and high bytes.
LOW(expr) and HIGH(expr) also extract bytes; PAGEOF(expr) extracts the
page of an address in 0 through 1023.
A lone $ or a unary * denotes the full current location.
Division truncates toward zero; shifts require nonnegative operands and
counts from 0 through 62. Overflow and division by zero are errors.
.SH PAGING
.TP
.BI "PAGE " n
Select page 0 through 3. Each page has an independent offset counter,
initially zero. Returning to a page resumes that counter.
.TP
.BI "ORG " offset
Set the current page's offset, from 0 through 255.
.TP
.BI "ORGA " address
Set both page and offset from an absolute address, from 0 through 1023.
.TP
.BR "WRAP ON" " / " "WRAP OFF"
Allow or reject emission wrapping from offset 255 to offset 0 of the same
page. Initially OFF; the setting persists across PAGE selections.
A single emitted block cannot exceed 256 bytes.
.PP
These directives do not emit instructions or change the hardware PG register.
Assembly never advances silently into the next page. Overlapping emitted or
reserved locations are errors, including overlaps due to wrapping.
.PP
Labels carry full addresses. JMP, BZ0, BZ1, BL0, and BL1 infer the encoded
page from a label. They also accept an explicit page,target pair, checked
for agreement with a full target. Numeric targets 0 through 255 are offsets
in the current assembly page; larger targets are absolute addresses.
.PP
JSR, BB0, BB1, BT0, BT1, LRM, LRS, SRM, and SRS require symbolic or absolute
targets on the current page. LOW(label) explicitly discards page information.
Arithmetic with one address retains its address provenance; subtracting two
addresses produces a scalar, as do extraction and bit operations.
.PP
JSR and RSR do not save or restore PG. Cross-page subroutine calls require
explicit jump stubs to enter and return from the other page.
.SH INSTRUCTION FORMS
.nf
  No operands:       NOP EIN DIN SEL REL SEZ REZ RSR
  op Rn:             CPY INR DCR AND XOR EXR ROR SHR RRL
                     JCR LRX LSX SRX SSX
  op Rn,#byte:       LRI XRI ANI
  op Rn,address:     LRM LRS SRM SRS
  op Rn,port:        LRD SRD
  op Rn,target:      BB0 BB1
  op Tn,target:      BT0 BT1
  op [page,]target:  JMP BZ0 BZ1 BL0 BL1
  JSR target
  op #byte:          SCS SCW
.fi
.SH MACROS AND INCLUDES
.nf
  MACRO COUNTDOWN count
          LRI R4,#\ecount
  %%loop: DCR R4
          BZ0 %%loop
  ENDM
          COUNTDOWN 3
.fi
Parameters use \ename or positional \e1, \e2, and so on.
Substitution is textual and does not occur inside strings or comments.
Parenthesize arguments when needed to preserve expression precedence.
%%name produces a unique local label per invocation; \e@ is the invocation
number. The __M namespace is reserved for generated names.
Macros must be defined before use, may call other macros, and may not
replace instruction or directive names. Nested definitions are unsupported.
.PP
INCLUDE "file.inc" searches relative to the source or macro-definition
file first, then the directories specified with \-I.
.PP
IF expression, IFDEF name, IFNDEF name, ELSE, and ENDIF provide conditional
assembly. Conditions use previously known numeric EQU constants and macro
arguments, not assembly addresses or forward definitions.
IFDEF and IFNDEF test known numeric constants and macro definitions.
Skipped branches do not open includes.
REPT count through ENDR repeats a block.
Standalone REPT blocks receive unique %% names; inside a macro these names
belong to the enclosing invocation. Use a helper macro for fresh labels
on each nested repetition.
.SH DATA AND CHECK DIRECTIVES
Directives may have a leading dot.
.TP
.BI "BYTE " values
Emit bytes or quoted strings. DB and ASCII are aliases.
.TP
.BI "WORD " values
Emit unsigned 16-bit words, little-endian. DW is an alias.
.TP
.BI "FILL " count[,value]
Emit repeated bytes; the value defaults to zero.
.TP
.BI "RES " count[,value]
Reserve locations. With a value, also initialize them.
.TP
.BI "ASSERT " expression[,"message"]
Check an expression after symbol resolution.
.TP
.B ERROR "message"
Stop with an error.
.TP
.B END
End the entire translation unit, including further preprocessing.
.PP
Strings and characters support \en, \er, \et, \e0, \e\e, \e', \e",
and \exHH (exactly two hexadecimal digits).
A label on a directive binds to the location before that directive;
place labels after PAGE or ORG to name the new location.
.SH OUTPUT FORMATS
Intel HEX contains absolute data records of at most 16 bytes and an EOF
record. Only initialized cells are emitted. No relocation or entry-point
records are emitted. Reset starts execution at page 0, offset 0.
.PP
Binary output is always 1024 bytes. Index page*256+offset holds the program
cell; holes and uninitialized RES ranges use the gap-fill byte.
No separate initialized storage-memory image is produced.
.PP
Requested outputs are opened only after assembly succeeds, preserving
previous results on source errors. Lexical path checks reject collisions
with source/include files and other outputs; symbolic and hard-link aliases
are not resolved.
.SH LIMITS
Physical source lines: 4095 bytes. Expanded lines: 65535 bytes.
Expanded lines/invocations: 100000. Preprocessing nesting: 32 levels.
Expression/EQU nesting: 128 levels. Operands/parameters: 64.
REPT iterations: 10000. UTF-8 byte-order marks and CRLF lines are accepted.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success; nonzero indicates an assembly or input/output error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
  cmc8asm program.asm -o program.hex
  cmc8asm program.asm -f bin -o program.bin --fill 0xFF
  cmc8asm program.asm -I include -l program.lst -m program.map
  cmc8asm program.asm -q -o -
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
The accompanying README.md contains a tutorial and worked paging and macro
examples. The examples directory includes a cross-page call trampoline.
